{"id": "q5", "claim": "c2", "kind": "claim", "form": "change-condition", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4"], "question": "In its RQ2 results, the paper contrasts how strongly capability (MMLU) correlates with R-Judge specificity versus R-Judge balanced accuracy. If the paper had substituted the specificity–MMLU estimate from its first batch of models for its final-panel value, would that contrast still support its conclusion about the specificity metric? What does the gap between the two estimates teach?", "answer": "No. The first-nine-model estimate (0.85) is larger than balanced accuracy's +0.71, so specificity would look at least as capability-confounded as balanced accuracy, not weakly related (+0.16 at n=20). The gap also shows that the confound estimate itself moves with the panel.", "rubric": ["Concludes the contrast would no longer show specificity as weakly capability-related, and might even reverse it", "Uses the specific values: early estimate about 0.85 versus final about +0.16, and balanced accuracy about +0.71", "Notes the lesson that the capability-confound estimate depends on the panel"]},
{"id": "q10", "claim": "c3", "kind": "claim", "form": "change-condition", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s8"], "question": "Suppose AgentHarm's behavior-level internal consistency in the paper had matched what the paper reports for its misalignment criterion. How would that affect the paper's argument that item-sampling error does not explain the ranking disagreement in Table 1?", "answer": "The argument would be weakened for one of Table 1's three columns. The misalignment criterion has only a Spearman–Brown value of 0.70 from two scenario halves and no rank-stability estimate, whereas the dismissal rests on every item-rich instrument reaching at least 0.96 split-half and at least 0.97 rank stability. AgentHarm's rankings could then partly be noise, so item sampling could explain part of the disagreement.", "rubric": ["Concludes the dismissal of item-sampling error would no longer hold, at least for AgentHarm's column of Table 1", "Contrasts the misalignment value (Spearman–Brown about 0.70, no rank stability) with the paper's at-least-0.96 / at-least-0.97 standard", "Explains that a noisy within-benchmark ranking could itself produce some of the disagreement"]},
{"id": "q11", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4"], "question": "A reader cites AgentHarm's most negative capability-controlled partial correlation in the paper's Table 2 as evidence that AgentHarm is an invalid safety instrument. Which outcome does that partial concern, and why does the paper's reasoning reject the inference?", "answer": "The partial concerns τ² task success (−0.23, not significant). Capability already explains task success well (ρ = +0.60), so the partial only asks whether AgentHarm predicts what capability leaves unexplained in an outcome that is not a safety construct. The paper says this null is not a verdict on the safety scores. AgentHarm's partial with jailbreak safety is +0.72.", "rubric": ["Identifies the outcome as τ²-bench task success, a held-out outcome that is not a safety outcome", "Explains that capability already strongly predicts task success (about +0.60), so a null partial does not indict AgentHarm", "Notes AgentHarm's strong partial (about +0.72) with the jailbreak safety outcome"]},
{"id": "q14", "claim": "c6", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s6", "s8"], "question": "Two of the paper's safety scores clear its pre-specified 0.40 effect-size threshold against one of its held-out criteria after controlling capability. A reader concludes that both are validated predictors of that criterion beyond capability. Give the paper's reasons for rejecting this, and say which of the two is especially fragile and why.", "answer": "Both are R-Judge specificity and InjecAgent robustness against misalignment safety, and the paper treats them as exploratory. Their CIs include zero, they fail Bonferroni, power is about 0.35, and the pre-specified prediction named AgentHarm. R-Judge is especially fragile because its partial drops to +0.19 under balanced accuracy and reverses to −0.46 under recall.", "rubric": ["States the associations are only exploratory or suggestive, not validated", "Gives at least two of: CIs include zero, fails Bonferroni, power about 0.35, the pre-specified prediction named AgentHarm instead", "Identifies R-Judge as fragile because its partial changes sign under a different R-Judge metric (negative under recall)"]},
{"id": "q15", "claim": "c5", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s8"], "question": "Apply the paper's pre-specified confirmation rule for its model-panel expansion to the expanded-panel capability–misalignment estimate. Does the estimate pass? Which statistical results keep the paper from concluding that the original correlation was spurious or has vanished?", "answer": "It does not pass. The rule required ρ ≤ −0.35 at p < 0.05, and the estimate is −0.16 with bootstrap p = 0.40. But the 95% CI [−0.54, +0.22] still contains the original −0.44, and the nested-bootstrap change (Δρ = +0.27, CI [−0.25, +0.78]) is not significant, so the paper reports a weaker estimate rather than a disappearance.", "rubric": ["States the rule (about ρ ≤ −0.35 with p < 0.05) is not met by the expanded estimate (about −0.16, p about 0.40)", "Notes the expanded CI still includes the original −0.44 and/or the nested-bootstrap between-panel change is not significant"]},
{"id": "q22", "claim": null, "kind": "integration", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s0", "s4", "s5", "s7"], "question": "A model card cites only 'R-Judge F1 = 0.70' as evidence that its agent is safe. Using the paper's constant-baseline result, how far is this score above a policy that does no safety reasoning, and why? Name one other element of the paper's minimum requirements for a safety claim that the card omits, and give a specific paper finding showing why it matters.", "answer": "The score is only about 0.01 above the always-unsafe baseline of 0.690, about the same margin as o3-mini's 0.702. This is because F1 gives no credit for correctly identified benign traces. The card also omits the target behavior: R-Judge does not predict jailbreak safety (partial −0.11). Alternatively it omits the model panel: the trade-off moved from −0.64 at n=7 to +0.02 at n=18.", "rubric": ["Computes that 0.70 is only about 0.01 above the paper's 0.690 constant baseline", "Explains that F1 ignores true negatives, so the baseline scores high without discriminating", "Names a missing element (target behavior or model panel) and links it to a specific finding, e.g., the R-Judge jailbreak partial near −0.11 or the −0.64 to +0.02 panel-size reversal"]},
{"id": "q23", "claim": "c4", "kind": "integration", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s6", "s8"], "question": "Suppose a tenth partial test were added to the family the paper uses for its Bonferroni correction in RQ3. What would the corrected threshold be? Would any result the paper treats as firm or as exploratory change status? Name the results involved and their p-values.", "answer": "The threshold would be 0.05/10 = 0.005. The capability crossover Δ and AgentHarm→jailbreak (both p < 0.001) still survive and stay firm. The R-Judge/InjecAgent–misalignment cells (p ≈ 0.05–0.09) still fail and stay exploratory, so nothing changes.", "rubric": ["Computes the new threshold as about 0.005 and concludes no classification changes", "Names the capability crossover and AgentHarm→jailbreak (p < 0.001) as surviving and firm", "Names the R-Judge/InjecAgent–misalignment cells (p about 0.05–0.09) as failing and exploratory"]},
{"id": "q24", "claim": "c3", "kind": "integration", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s8"], "question": "The paper reports random-subsampling results for two quantities: the RQ1 R-Judge-specificity/AgentHarm-safety correlation and the capability interaction Δ. Using the specific subsample sizes and rates the paper gives, contrast how each behaved, and say what each result implies about that quantity.", "answer": "The R-Judge–AgentHarm correlation reached |ρ| ≥ 0.5 in about 25% of size-7 draws, even though its full-panel value is about +0.02. A strong-looking trade-off can therefore come from sampling alone, so it is an artifact. Δ was negative in 100% of size-10, -12 and -14 draws, with no sign flips and a median of about −0.95, so it is robust rather than a small-panel artifact.", "rubric": ["Reports the RQ1 correlation reaching |ρ| ≥ 0.5 in about a quarter of size-7 subsets despite a near-zero full-panel value", "Reports Δ negative in all size-10 to size-14 subsets with no sign flips (median about −0.95)", "Draws the contrast: the trade-off is a sampling artifact while Δ is stable"]}
]}
